Objective (s): TO PREPARE THE SOFTWARE REQUIREMENTS SPECIFICATION (SRS) FOR THE HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM WITH 28 CORE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS.
1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose : This SRS defines the functional and nonfunctional requirements for a small academic Hospital Management System (HMS). The system demonstrates practical software engineering concepts through a manageable web application developed by a single student. It also provides a consistent basis for the Use Case, DFD, Activity Diagram, ER Diagram, database design, implementation, and testing.
1.2 Document Conventions :
•	FR-xx denotes a functional requirement.
•	PR-xx denotes a performance requirement.
•	SEC-xx denotes a security requirement.
•	Priority levels are High, Medium, or Low.
•	The term 'system' refers to the Hospital Management System described in this document.
1.3 Intended Audience : The primary audience is the Software Engineering professor/evaluator and the student developer. Readers should first review Sections 1 and 2 for scope, then Section 3 for interfaces, Section 4 for testable functional requirements, Section 5 for quality constraints, and Section 6 for the project plan.
1.4 Product Scope : HMS will provide basic hospital administration functions: user login and role management, patient records, doctor and department records, appointments, admission/discharge records, prescriptions and medicine records, billing/payment status, dashboard/reporting, and two simple advisory AI functions. The AI functions are academic decision-support features and are not intended to diagnose disease or replace a medical professional.
2. Overall Description
2.1 Product Perspective : HMS is a browser-based client-server application. The front end uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript. The back end uses Java with Spring Boot and exposes application services/API endpoints. MySQL stores users, patients, doctors, departments, appointments, admissions, prescriptions, medicines, bills and related data.
2.2 Product Functions :
•	Authenticate users and apply role-based access.
•	Create, search, view and update patient information.
•	Maintain doctor, department and availability information.
•	Book, update, cancel and track appointments.
•	Record patient admission, ward/bed information and discharge.
•	Create prescriptions and maintain medicine/stock records.
•	Generate bills, calculate totals and record payment status.
•	Display dashboard summaries and basic reports.
•	Suggest a department from selected symptoms using a simple rule/score approach.
•	Suggest appointment priority using predefined severity/priority rules.
2.3 User Classes and Characteristics :
User Class	Main Responsibilities
Admin	Manage users/roles and maintain master data such as doctors and departments.
Doctor	View relevant patient information, manage prescriptions and update appointment-related status.
Receptionist	Register patients, manage appointments, admissions/discharges and billing activities.
Patient	View basic appointment/patient-related information if the implemented UI provides patient access.

2.4 Operating Environment :
•	Client: modern web browser such as Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge or Mozilla Firefox.
•	Server: Java Runtime Environment with Spring Boot.
•	Database server: MySQL.
•	Development environment: Windows/Linux/macOS with Java IDE and MySQL.
•	Network: local development network or standard HTTP/HTTPS web access.
2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints :
•	The project is developed by one student, so functionality is intentionally limited to a practical academic scope.
•	Backend must use Java and Spring Boot.
•	Frontend must use HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
•	MySQL is the selected database.
•	AI must remain simple, explainable and advisory; no clinical diagnosis or complex machine-learning pipeline is required.
•	The system is an academic prototype and is not a certified hospital EHR/clinical platform.
2.6 User Documentation : A concise user guide/readme will describe login, patient registration, appointment booking, admission/discharge, prescription, billing, and dashboard usage. Basic setup instructions for Java, Spring Boot and MySQL will also be included.
2.7 Assumptions and Dependencies :
•	Users have valid credentials and appropriate roles.
•	The MySQL database is available during normal system use.
•	Doctors, departments, medicines and other master data are entered correctly by authorized users.
•	The AI suggestions are based on predefined academic rules and are not medical advice.
•	The project will use a manageable dataset suitable for demonstration and testing.
3. External Interface Requirements
3.1 User Interfaces :
•	Login page with username/email and password fields.
•	Role-based dashboard after successful authentication.
•	Forms for patients, doctors, departments, appointments, admissions, prescriptions, medicines and bills.
•	Search/filter controls for patient, doctor and appointment records.
•	Validation messages for missing, invalid or conflicting input.
•	Simple tables/cards for displaying records and dashboard summaries.
3.2 Hardware Interfaces : No specialized hardware is required. A standard desktop/laptop with keyboard, mouse, display, and network connectivity is sufficient.
3.3 Software Interfaces :
•	Java/JDK and Spring Boot for server-side application logic.
•	MySQL for persistent data storage.
•	Web browser for the user interface.
•	JavaScript for client-side interaction and validation where required.
3.4 Communications Interfaces : The browser communicates with the Spring Boot application using HTTP/HTTPS. The application communicates with MySQL using the configured database driver and connection settings.
4. System Features
The following eight features contain exactly 28 core functional requirements, scoped to match the diagrams and the implementation capacity of a solo developer.
4.1 Authentication and Role Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: User enters valid credentials → system verifies them → dashboard is displayed. Invalid credentials → error message.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-01	The system shall authenticate a registered user using valid login credentials.
FR-02	The system shall provide role-based access for Admin, Doctor and Receptionist functions.
FR-03	The system shall allow an authenticated user to log out and end the active session.

4.2 Patient Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: User opens patient module → enters/searches patient data → system validates → record is created, displayed or updated.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-04	The system shall allow an authorized user to create a patient record with required personal and contact details.
FR-05	The system shall allow an authorized user to search and view patient records.
FR-06	The system shall allow an authorized user to update an existing patient record.
FR-07	The system shall validate required patient fields before saving a record.

4.3 Doctor and Department Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: Admin/receptionist manages doctor/department data → system stores changes → available doctors are shown for appointment use.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-08	The system shall allow an authorized user to add and maintain doctor records.
FR-09	The system shall allow an authorized user to create and maintain department records.
FR-10	The system shall display doctor availability/listing information for appointment selection.

4.4 Appointment Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: User selects patient, doctor and time → system checks availability → appointment is confirmed or conflict is reported.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-11	The system shall allow an authorized user to create an appointment for a patient with a selected doctor.
FR-12	The system shall check doctor/date-time availability before confirming an appointment.
FR-13	The system shall allow an authorized user to reschedule or cancel an appointment.
FR-14	The system shall maintain an appointment status such as Scheduled, Completed or Cancelled.

4.5 Admission and Discharge Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: Patient selected → admission details entered → ward/bed/status stored → later discharge details recorded and admission closed.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-15	The system shall allow an authorized user to create an admission record for a patient.
FR-16	The system shall allow authorized users to update ward/bed and admission status information.
FR-17	The system shall create a discharge record containing discharge date and basic discharge details.

4.6 Prescription and Medicine Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: Doctor selects patient → enters medicines/instructions → system validates → prescription is stored and available for viewing.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-18	The system shall allow a doctor to create a prescription for a patient.
FR-19	The system shall allow authorized users to maintain medicine records.
FR-20	The system shall allow authorized users to view and update basic medicine stock information.
FR-21	The system shall validate required prescription and medicine fields before saving.

4.7 Billing and Payment Management
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: High.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: User selects patient → enters bill items → system calculates total → payment status is recorded.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-22	The system shall allow an authorized user to create a bill for a patient.
FR-23	The system shall calculate the bill total from the entered bill items/charges.
FR-24	The system shall record payment status for a bill, such as Pending or Paid.

4.8 Dashboard, Reporting and Simple AI Support
Description and Priority: This feature is part of the core HMS scope. Priority: Medium.
Stimulus/Response Sequences: User opens dashboard → summaries/reports are displayed. User enters selected symptoms → department suggestion is returned. User enters priority inputs → Low/Medium/High suggestion is returned.
ID	Functional Requirement
FR-25	The system shall display dashboard summaries such as patient, appointment, admission and billing counts.
FR-26	The system shall provide at least one basic report/list view for administrative review.
FR-27	The system shall suggest a suitable hospital department from selected symptoms using a predefined rule/score-based algorithm.
FR-28	The system shall suggest an appointment priority level using predefined academic priority rules.

5. Nonfunctional Requirements
5.1 Performance Requirements :
ID	Requirement
PR-01	For normal academic demonstration loads, common pages should respond within approximately 3 seconds.
PR-02	Database searches for patient, doctor and appointment records should return results without unnecessary delay.
PR-03	The application should avoid duplicate appointment bookings caused by a conflicting doctor/date-time selection.
PR-04	Dashboard summaries should load within a reasonable time for the expected project dataset.

5.2 Safety Requirements :
•	The AI features shall be presented as advisory suggestions only.
•	The system shall not claim that an AI suggestion is a medical diagnosis.
•	Required input validation should reduce accidental creation of incomplete records.
•	The system should prevent or clearly warn about conflicting appointment data.
5.3 Security Requirements :
ID	Requirement
SEC-01	Passwords shall not be stored as plain text; a secure password-hashing mechanism should be used.
SEC-02	Role-based access shall restrict functions to authorized users.
SEC-03	Unauthenticated users shall not access protected application pages/APIs.
SEC-04	User input shall be validated before database operations.
SEC-05	Database credentials shall not be hard-coded into publicly visible frontend files.
SEC-06	Sensitive session/authentication information shall be handled using the selected Spring Boot security approach.
SEC-07	Only authorized users shall be able to modify critical patient, appointment, prescription and billing records.

5.4 Software Quality Attributes :
•	Usability: Forms and navigation should be simple enough for demonstration users to understand without extensive training.
•	Maintainability: Backend modules, controllers/services/repositories and frontend pages should be organized logically.
•	Reliability: Invalid operations should produce clear errors rather than silently corrupting records.
•	Scalability: The design should allow additional modules later, but such modules are outside the current project scope.
•	Testability: Core functions should be testable using sample users and sample hospital data.
