Experiment No. 3
Objective (s): TO IDENTIFY THE ACTORS AND MAJOR FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS OF THE HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM USING A UML USE CASE DIAGRAM.
•	INTRODUCTION TO USE CASE DIAGRAM :-
A Use Case Diagram is a UML behavioral diagram used to represent the functional requirements of a software system. It describes the interaction between external actors and the use cases provided by the software. Actors represent users or external systems, while use cases represent services or tasks performed by the software. The diagram helps to show what the system does from the user's point of view.
•	COMPONENTS OF A USE CASE DIAGRAM :-
Component	Description
Actor	An external entity (person or system) that interacts with the system, shown as a stick figure.
Use Case	A function or service the system provides to an actor, shown as an ellipse.
System Boundary	A rectangle that separates what is inside the system from what is outside it.
Association	A straight line connecting an actor to a use case.
<<include>>	Dotted arrow showing reusable mandatory behavior: one use case always uses another.
<<extend>>	Dotted arrow showing optional or conditional behavior added to a use case.

•	USE CASE DIAGRAM FOR HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM :-
 
Fig. 3.1 Use Case Diagram of Hospital Management System
•	EXPLANATION :-
Actors in the diagram :-
1) Admin : Manage Users, Manage Departments, View Reports, Login and Logout.
2) Receptionist : Register Patient, Search Patient, Update Patient Record and Book Appointment.
3) Doctor : Manage Doctors, View Doctor Schedule, check availability and handle appointment-related activities.
4) Patient : View Appointment, Admit Patient, View Admission Details and Discharge Patient.
5) AI System : Suggest Department based on symptoms and suggest appointment priority based on symptoms/severity as advisory functions.
Main functional areas :-
1) User & Department Management: Admin manages users and hospital departments.
2) Patient Management: Receptionist registers, searches and updates patient records.
3) Appointment Management: Appointments can be booked, checked, rescheduled, cancelled and tracked.
4) Admission & Discharge: Patient admission, details and discharge are managed through the system.
5) Prescription & Medicine: Doctors can create/view prescriptions and medicine stock/list information can be managed.
6) Billing & Payment: Bills are generated, details are viewed, payments are made and payment status can be checked.
7) Analytics & Reports: Dashboard and reports support hospital management and analysis.
8) AI Advisory Functions: The AI System suggests a suitable department and appointment priority based on symptoms/severity. These functions are advisory and are not medical diagnosis.
Relationships in the diagram :-
•	Login:  Manage Users, Manage Departments and View Reports include Login.
•	Logout:  Logout extends Login.
•	Validation:  Register Patient and Update Patient Record include Validate Patient Data.
•	Appointments:  Book Appointment includes Appointment Management, which includes Check Doctor Availability, Reschedule Appointment, Cancel Appointment and View Appointment Status.
•	Prescription:  Create Prescription is included in Manage Prescription & Medicine along with Manage Medicine Stock and View Medicine List.
•	Billing:  Generate Bill and Make Payment are included in Manage Billing; View Payment Status extends Manage Billing.
•	Analytics & AI:  View Dashboard and Generate Reports are included in View Analytics; Suggest Department and Suggest Priority extend View Analytics and are linked to the AI System.
•	CONCLUSION: The Use Case Diagram clearly represents the functional requirements of the Hospital Management System and the interaction of its major actors with the system. It provides a clear foundation for designing and developing the HMS by identifying responsibilities, major services, and relationships among use cases.
Experiment No. 4
Objective (s): TO MODEL THE APPOINTMENT BOOKING WORKFLOW OF THE HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM USING A UML ACTIVITY DIAGRAM WITH SWIMLANES.
•	INTRODUCTION TO ACTIVITY DIAGRAM :-
An Activity Diagram is a UML behavioral diagram used to describe the flow of activities and decisions in a software process. It shows the starting point, actions, decision conditions, alternative flows, responsibilities, and final state. Swimlanes identify which actor or system component performs each activity.
•	COMPONENTS OF AN ACTIVITY DIAGRAM :-
Component	Description
Initial Node	A filled black circle that shows where the process starts.
Activity / Action	A rounded rectangle that represents one step performed in the process.
Decision Node	A diamond that splits the flow based on a condition (for example Yes / No).
Control Flow	An arrow that connects activities and shows the order of execution.
Swimlane	A vertical lane that shows which actor or component is responsible for an activity.
Final Node	A circle with a border that shows where the process ends.

•	ACTIVITY DIAGRAM FOR HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM (APPOINTMENT BOOKING) :-
Process: Appointment Booking and Confirmation.   Swimlanes: Patient, System, Doctor, Database.
 
Fig. 4.1 Activity Diagram - Appointment Booking
•	EXPLANATION :-
Activity flow description :-
Step	Activity / Description
1	Patient logs in and selects Book Appointment.
2	Patient enters symptoms or selects doctor/department and views available doctors and time slots.
3	System validates patient details. If invalid, an error message is shown and the flow returns for correction.
4	If valid, the system checks doctor availability and the selected time slot.
5	If the slot is unavailable, alternative slots are shown. If available, the appointment record is saved.
6	Database stores the appointment data.
7	System sends confirmation notification and patient views appointment details and confirmation.
8	Doctor views appointment requests, accepts/rejects the appointment, and the status is updated in the system and database.

Key decision points :-
•	Patient valid? - No: show invalid-patient error and return to input/selection. Yes: continue to availability checking.
•	Slot available? - No: show alternative slots. Yes: save appointment record and continue with confirmation.
•	CONCLUSION: The Activity Diagram clearly represents the appointment booking process of the Hospital Management System and shows coordination among the patient, system, doctor, and database for validation, availability, storage, status update, and confirmation.
